Live two-home multi-profile proof — real Hermes 0.22.5, plugin built from
this worktree (branch feat/multi-profile), local backend.

Homes (scratch, deleted after the run):
  A = <work>/roam-homes/mp-a   (device dev-a)
  B = <work>/roam-homes/mp-b   (device dev-b)
Shared store: <work>/roam-homes/mp-store (local backend)
Fleet archive key: generated per `hermes roam init`'s instructions, written
to both homes' .env, never printed.

Every command ran as
  cd <work> && HERMES_RUNTIME_DIR=<install tools> HERMES_HOME=<home> hermes ...

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Real profile creation on A (per-profile SOUL/memory/skill/config)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ hermes profile create bot1 --no-skills --description "support bot one"
$ hermes profile create bot2 --no-skills --description "support bot two"
  ...
  Edit ~/work/roam-homes/mp-a/profiles/bot1/SOUL.md to customize personality

$ hermes profile list
  Profile          Model    Gateway   Alias   Distribution
  ◆default         —        stopped   —       —
   bot1            —        stopped   bot1    —
   bot2            —        stopped   bot2    —

$ ls -a profiles/bot1/
  .env  .no-bundled-skills  SOUL.md  cron  home  logs  memories  plans
  profile.yaml  sessions  skills  skins  workspace

$ cat profiles/bot1/profile.yaml
  description: support bot one
  description_auto: false

Per-profile content added by hand (bot1 shown; bot2 analogous):
  profiles/bot1/SOUL.md, profiles/bot1/memories/MEMORY.md,
  profiles/bot1/skills/bot1-greeter/SKILL.md, profiles/bot1/config.yaml

Plugin installed from the worktree on both homes:
  hermes plugins install file://<work>/hermes-roam-lanes/profiles --yes
  hermes plugins enable hermes-roam

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Push from the ROOT home of A
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ hermes roam init --create-remote
  roam: created the generation-0 remote head on the local backend and registered device 'dev-a'
  ...
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-a — profiles: default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced)

$ hermes roam push
  roam: pushed generation 1 (15 files, 4613 bytes)
  roam: bundle bundles/000001-dev-a-61abe28cd95b.roam
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-a — profiles: default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced)
  roam: published 0 session transcript(s) covering profile(s) none in this device's read-only archive namespace (archive/sessions/dev-a/)

  The session-archive export loop ran Hermes's real `hermes sessions export`
  once per home of the root (root as profile 'default', then bot1 and bot2);
  the fresh scratch homes hold no sessions, so every export returned zero
  transcripts. A failed export would have degraded the push with the fixed
  "could not be exported ... keeps the previously published archive" notice
  instead; none did.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Pull on a fresh second home B: profiles arrive working and listable
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ hermes roam pull            (plan)
  would add shared profiles/bot1/SOUL.md
  would add shared profiles/bot1/config.yaml
  would add shared profiles/bot1/memories/MEMORY.md
  would add shared profiles/bot1/profile.yaml
  would add shared profiles/bot1/skills/autonomous-ai-agents/DESCRIPTION.md
  would add shared profiles/bot1/skills/bot1-greeter/SKILL.md
  would add shared profiles/bot2/SOUL.md
  ... (same set for bot2)
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-b — profiles: default(synced)

$ hermes roam pull --apply
  backup  <work>/roam-homes/mp-b/roam/backups/20261107-190529
  roam: 10 file(s) written, 0 deleted

$ hermes profile list
  Profile          Model    Gateway   Alias   Distribution
  ◆default         —        stopped   —       —
   bot1            —        stopped   bot1    —
   bot2            —        stopped   bot2    —            <-- arrived, listable

$ cat profiles/bot1/profile.yaml
  description: support bot one
  description_auto: false
$ ls profiles/bot1/
  SOUL.md  memories  profile.yaml  skills
$ cat profiles/bot2/memories/MEMORY.md
  bot2 remembers: always cite evidence.

  profiles/<name>/config.yaml follows the ruled config policy (host-scoped
  keys stay local; a locally-changed config is never overwritten), exactly
  like the top-level config.yaml. Per-profile .env stayed local: the fleet
  key had to be (and was) provisioned in B's profile .env by hand.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Roam INSIDE a profile context maps up to the ROOT home
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(plugin installed into profiles/bot1 as a real cloned-profile deployment has)

$ HERMES_HOME=<work>/roam-homes/mp-b/profiles/bot1 hermes roam status
  device       dev-b
  root         <work>/roam-homes/mp-b        <-- the ROOT home, not the profile
  backend      local
  profiles     default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced)
  pushed       generation 0
  pulled       generation 1
  remote head  generation 1 by dev-a

$ hermes -p bot1 roam status
  device       dev-b
  root         <work>/roam-homes/mp-b        <-- same root, same lock
  profiles     default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced)

  The device name and every sync setting come from the ROOT home's
  config.yaml; the profile's own config.yaml is never consulted for them.
  Both profile-context forms share the ROOT home's roam lock, so concurrent
  bots can never interleave generations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Per-profile exclusion is disclosed at every surface
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ printf 'local:\n  - profiles/bot2/\n' > roam/rules.yaml   (on B)
$ hermes roam status
  root      <work>/roam-homes/mp-b
  profiles  default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(excluded)   <-- verdict shown

$ hermes roam rules | tail -3
  profiles and bots: every profiles/<name>/ travels as one tree with the root
  home (profile.yaml, config.yaml, SOUL.md, memories/, skills/, skins/,
  hermes-pack.yaml shared; .env, auth.json, state.db and sessions/ local per
  profile) — exclude one with a local: rule for profiles/<name>/ in this file
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-b — profiles: default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(excluded)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. A profile that exists on one device only arrives on the other
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(on A) $ hermes profile create bot3 --no-skills --description "third bot, one device only"
        $ hermes roam push
  roam: pushed generation 2 (18 files, 5480 bytes)
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-a — profiles: default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced), bot3(synced)

(on B) $ hermes roam pull --apply
  roam: 3 file(s) written, 0 deleted
        $ hermes profile list
  ◆default
   bot1  bot2  bot3                                     <-- bot3 arrived, listable
        $ cat profiles/bot3/SOUL.md
  You are bot3, created only on mp-a.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Deleting the profile on A propagates the deletion to B
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(on A) $ hermes profile delete bot3   (confirmed interactively)
  ✓ Removed <work>/roam-homes/mp-a/profiles/bot3
        $ hermes roam push
  roam: pushed generation 3 (15 files, 4650 bytes)
  roam: covered root <work>/roam-homes/mp-a — profiles: default(synced), bot1(synced), bot2(synced)

(on B) $ hermes roam pull --apply
  roam: 0 file(s) written, 3 deleted
        $ hermes profile list
  ◆default
   bot1  bot2                                          <-- bot3 is gone and unlistable

  Only an empty skills/ directory remains under B's profiles/bot3/ (file-level
  sync deletes files, not directories); the profile is no longer listable
  because its identity files are gone. B never excluded the profile, so the
  deletion by the device that could see it carried everywhere — the
  carry-forward tests pin that a device that DID exclude a profile can never
  delete it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. Health and the sessions surface
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ hermes roam doctor
  roam: 9/9 checks passed

$ hermes roam sessions
  roam: no pulled session archives on this device
  roam: a device that enables sync_sessions publishes its transcripts (every profile of its root home); run `hermes roam pull --apply` to receive them

  (no scratch home ever held a real session, so the live archive stayed
  empty; the per-profile export, cap, namespace, listing, and read paths are
  covered by tests/test_multi_profile.py and tests/test_session_archive.py)
